WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.000
A new type of war has emerged, information
warfare.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.200
This activity by the Russians is gonna go
down in history

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:11.360
as the greatest covert action campaign in
the history of Mother Russia.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:14.920
So-called fake news can have real world consequences

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:17.340
it's a danger that must be addressed.

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:21.560
And you have to be the ones to do something
about it

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:22.500
or we will.

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:26.500
Last October, both houses of Congress

00:00:26.509 --> 00:00:30.179
grilled the attorneys for the tech giants
Facebook, Twitter and Google

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:33.660
about Russian meddling and so-called fake
news.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:36.840
You have been identified as the major distributors
of fake news.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.840
We are not gonna go away, gentlemen.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:44.300
Minnesota Senator Amy Klobuchar has already
introduced a piece of legislation

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:47.840
aimed at more tightly regulating online political
advertising.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:49.700
The Honest Ads Act

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:53.960
would bring transparency and accountability
to paid political advertising.

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:57.420
But that only addresses a small portion of
the problem.

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:00.800
A Russian troll farm called the Internet Research
Agency

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:05.870
produced thousands of unpaid posts using fake
accounts and Facebook groups

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:09.320
that targeted hot button issues like race
and immigration.

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:15.100
Their strategy is to take a crack in our society
and turn it into a chasm.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:17.480
And it's not just foreign misinformation,

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:20.680
some lawmakers want better policing of sites
like Info Wars,

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:25.120
the Austin Texas based conspiracy site run
by Alex Jones.

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:27.020
A short time ago on Twitter,

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:30.620
if you clicked on the hashtag #NYCTerroristAttack,

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:34.250
the top tweet links to an Info Wars story
with the headline:

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:40.140
'Imam: I warned De Blasio About NYC Terror;
He Was Too Busy Bashing Trump.'

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:46.510
Do you have a responsibility to flag something
as this was fake news?

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:49.990
We see our users do that a lot, we are an open public
platform with-

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:52.720
You must feel like you have some responsibility.

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:56.840
We are deeply concerned about that and figuring
out ways we can do it with the right balance.

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:58.400
While Democrats seemed concerned

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:02.020
that tech companies don't do enough to police
content on their platforms,

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:04.640
Republicans and Conservatives have expressed
concern

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:08.200
that they do too much to cultivate their users'
news feeds.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:10.560
Your power sometimes scares me.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:15.140
Do you consider your sites to be a neutral
public forum?

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:18.329
Google should be regulated like the public
utility it is

00:02:18.329 --> 00:02:22.150
to make sure it doesn't further distort the
free flow of information.

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:25.659
With both sides increasingly suspicious of
big tech

00:02:25.659 --> 00:02:29.120
the regulation of online speech seems imminent.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:33.659
The specter of fake news and foreign propaganda
posing as factual reporting

00:02:33.659 --> 00:02:37.849
is a complicated problem that many lawmakers
seem eager to tackle.

00:02:37.849 --> 00:02:41.470
But as President Trump demonstrates on a near
daily basis:

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:45.720
what constitutes fake news is often in the
eye of the beholder.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:47.800
You are fake news, go ahead.

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:51.940
This isn't to deny the existence or importance
of things like facts

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:56.790
but to say that handing the authority to determine
truth over to government officials

00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:58.420
could be disastrous.

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:02.159
It's important to remember that being on Facebook
or Twitter or YouTube

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:04.520
is still a voluntary activity.

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:06.920
Because customers can always log off,

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:09.480
these companies already have strong incentives

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:12.780
to keep their platforms relatively free of
bots and trolls

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:17.800
and to improve the user experience through
actively curating content.

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:23.520
What we're trying to do actually is provide
each user a personalized news feed

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:27.980
that will be the content that's most interesting
to that user.

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:33.519
Treating social media as some sort of public
utility is quite simply a power grab

00:03:33.519 --> 00:03:36.879
that all but guarantees politicians and un-elected
bureaucrats

00:03:36.879 --> 00:03:39.870
will decide what information should appear
in Americans' news feeds

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:44.329
and would likely grant the government even
greater access to our private communications

00:03:44.329 --> 00:03:46.080
than it already has.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:47.780
Are you intending to turn over to the committee

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:51.180
any kind of direct messaging that went on
among the different accounts.

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:54.300
Direct messages are the private communication
between our users

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:59.140
and so we take that privacy right and responsibility
very seriously.

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:03.920
We don't want to put ourselves in the position
of being the arbiter of truth

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:07.900
we don't think that's a tenable position for
any company or industry.

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:10.320
It's probably true that Facebook, Twitter
and Google

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:13.360
aren't in a great position to be the arbiters of truth.

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:16.180
But the government is in an even worse position.

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:20.780
This isn't the first time governments have
tried to control new tools of mass communication.

00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:25.199
Much like the internet, the advent of the
printing press provoked panic and backlash

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:27.680
among the elite institutions it disrupted.

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:31.520
America's first public newspaper was shut
down after a single edition

00:04:31.530 --> 00:04:34.970
because it spread rumors about the sex lives
of government officials

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:38.960
and published what the colonial government
described as 'uncertain reports.'

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:41.260
or what we might call 'fake news.'

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:43.260
And for the crime of publishing without a
license

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:48.650
the government imprisoned and later ran out
of town another early colonial newspaper editor,

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:53.729
James Franklin, older brother to Benjamin
Franklin, who went on to run that paper.

00:04:53.729 --> 00:04:57.080
A few decades earlier, John Milton famously
criticized

00:04:57.080 --> 00:05:00.780
the British government's regulation of materials
produced by the printing press,

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:02.660
Writing in 1644

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:05.840
that 'Truth and Understanding are not such wares

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:09.580
as to be monopolized and traded in by tickets and statute-'

00:05:09.590 --> 00:05:13.680
Instead, better to 'Let Truth and Falsehood
grapple.'

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:18.040
Why was this false content allowed to remain
in place?

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:22.080
There was a small amount of tweets relative
to the size of the platform

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:28.040
but impressions of tweets calling out those
things as fake were 8 times as large.

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:31.100
Granting government even the slightest control

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:35.320
over the free flow of information on the internet
under the guise of fighting falsehoods

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:39.700
will ironically make more difficult the task
of discovering truth.

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:42.819
Some level of fake news, misinformation and
trolling

00:05:42.820 --> 00:05:46.580
might just be the price we have to pay for
free and open discussion.

